---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="About the IHHS Study Guide Hub.">
  <section class="container-prose py-16 lg:py-20">
    <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-ink-500 font-semibold mb-3">About</div>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight mb-6">
      A study tool that <span class="gradient-text">actually helps</span>
    </h1>

    <figure class="not-prose my-10 rounded-2xl border-l-4 border-accent-600 bg-gradient-to-br from-accent-50 to-white dark:from-accent-900/20 dark:to-ink-950 p-8 shadow-soft">
      <blockquote class="font-serif text-xl sm:text-2xl italic leading-relaxed text-ink-900 dark:text-ink-50 text-balance">
        &ldquo;The greatest lives are the ones willing to be forgotten so something else can be remembered.&rdquo;
      </blockquote>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose dark:prose-invert">
      <p>
        The IHHS Study Guide Hub is a free, open-source, ad-free library of deep-dive study guides,
        built by an IHHS student for IHHS students. Accounts are optional. Every guide is structured
        the way memory actually works: learning objectives first, then the core concepts, then worked
        examples, then active recall through quizzes and flashcards.
      </p>
      <p>
        Classmates who have used the guides and vouch for them include Owin Abeysinghe, Sayan Bhandari,
        Kai Mcparlane, Ethan Periera, Colin Nielson, and Oliver Netherwood. Work through a guide properly
        and the test tends to take care of itself. If it still doesn't, that one is a skill issue.
      </p>

      <h2>Promises</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>The site will never cost money and will never carry ads.</li>
        <li>Your information is never sold or shared. The <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a> spells out exactly what is stored and where.</li>
        <li>Nothing is held back. Every guide uses the most effective tools available for learning, not a watered-down version of them.</li>
        <li>Good ideas get built. Suggest something sensible and there is a good chance it ends up on the site.</li>
        <li>The project is non-profit and fully open source, and it will stay that way. The code lives on <a href="https://github.com/notkyler0601-dev/ihhs-study-guide-hub">GitHub</a>.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What every guide includes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Learning objectives</strong>, so you know what mastery looks like before you start</li>
        <li><strong>A glossary</strong> of every term you will meet</li>
        <li><strong>Core concepts in sections</strong>, each with worked examples</li>
        <li><strong>Diagrams, maps, and equations</strong> rendered properly, many of them interactive</li>
        <li><strong>Practice questions</strong> with collapsible answers</li>
        <li><strong>A self-quiz</strong> with instant explanations</li>
        <li><strong>A flashcard deck</strong> with shuffle and progress tracking</li>
        <li><strong>Mnemonics, common pitfalls, and a one-page cheat sheet</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to use it</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Browse the library, or search from anywhere with <span class="kbd">⌘K</span> / <span class="kbd">Ctrl K</span></li>
        <li>Read the guide end to end</li>
        <li>Take the self-quiz and read the explanations for anything you missed</li>
        <li>Drill the flashcards until they feel effortless</li>
        <li>Print the cheat sheet for the night before</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>How to contribute</h2>
      <p>
        Found a mistake? Want to write a guide for a class that doesn't have one yet? Talk to the
        maintainer directly. Guides are plain markdown files, so anyone who can write a Google Doc
        can write one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
